<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      The dark set's launcher and splash colours, restating DarkFlickColors the way
      values/colors.xml restates the light one. The two brand roles trade places here
      because the palette does: the mark is the action gold, the bars are the accent blue.

      These reach TWO surfaces, and only one of them can follow the in-app preference.

      The API 31+ SPLASH does. values-night-v31 names @drawable/ic_launcher_foreground,
      which reads the two colours below, and it is resolved inside this app's own process —
      where the per-app night mode MainActivity stores through UiModeManager applies. So a
      user who chooses Dark in Settings gets a gold cold-start mark from the next launch on,
      at 12.46:1 against that bucket's #0C0F18 plate. That is the strongest answer this
      platform has to "make the logo follow the choice permanently".

      The HOME-SCREEN ICON does not, and cannot. The adaptive icon is inflated by whoever
      DISPLAYS it: the launcher builds a Resources object for our package inside its own
      process, so the -night qualifier is matched against the LAUNCHER's configuration —
      the system's night mode, never ours. Android has no supported light/dark variant of a
      launcher icon at all; the documented mechanism is the <monochrome> layer, which
      ic_launcher_monochrome.xml already ships and the launcher tints itself. Worse, AOSP's
      shared IconProvider invalidates its icon cache on ACTION_OVERLAY_CHANGED only, so even
      a SYSTEM dark-mode toggle does not repaint the cached bitmap — the night icon lands on
      the next cache rebuild (package update, reinstall, eviction), not on the flip. Ship
      this as a best-effort improvement; do not promise it is instant.

      The activity-alias swap is the technique that would close that gap, and it is
      deliberately NOT implemented, so that this does not get re-litigated: disabling an
      alias forces process death and DONT_KILL_APP is ignored for that transition. This
      process owns CastServerService — the foreground byte-range server, the control socket
      and the playback session — so the cost of a home-screen repaint is a stall on the TV
      mid-cast, which is the one failure this whole product exists to prevent. It is also
      unofficial and launcher-dependent, minSdk here is 26 where the icon can vanish from
      the home screen outright, and the flick:// pairing filter would have to be duplicated
      on every alias.
    -->
    <!-- DarkFlickColors.surfaceRaised — a dark slate that lets the launcher-owned shape
         remain distinct from both dark wallpaper and the near-black app canvas. -->
    <color name="ic_launcher_background">#FF171D2E</color>
    <!-- The action gold: 10.92:1 on that background, 12.46:1 on the #0C0F18 splash plate. -->
    <color name="ic_launcher_mark">#FFFFC93D</color>
    <!-- The accent blue: 2.23:1 and 3.87:1 on that background at the drawable's 0.45/0.75
         strokeAlpha. Decorative, exactly as in FlickMark — the triangle is the mark. -->
    <color name="ic_launcher_spark">#FF6E93FF</color>
    <!-- ic_launcher_monochrome is deliberately absent, so the default bucket supplies it:
         the launcher re-tints that layer itself and must get only its alpha. -->
</resources>
